  "claims": [
    {
      "statement": "Affective polarization has been rising rapidly worldwide in recent years on a global-average basis.",
      "source": "WTW Political Risk Index H1 2025"
    },
    {
      "statement": "On the V-Dem-based measure, global affective polarization has shot up since around 2000 to levels unprecedented in modern history.",
      "source": "WTW Political Risk Index H1 2025"
    },
    {
      "statement": "Surges in polarization in democracies tend to be accompanied by the rise of populist movements and increased political violence.",
      "source": "WTW Political Risk Index H1 2025"
    },
    {
      "statement": "Truth-and-reconciliation processes (Uruguay, Timor-Leste, Chile) and international war (post-WWII Norway, Luxembourg, Denmark; Ukraine) have historically reduced polarization.",
      "source": "WTW Political Risk Index H1 2025"
    },
    {
      "statement": "The WTW measure relies on expert assessments (V-Dem, Fragile States Index) rather than opinion polls, which the report itself notes is probably less accurate than poll-based measures.",
